    "summary": "The Somali-registered vessel TEDY was attacked near the Ukrainian port of Odesa in the Black Sea, resulting in the deaths of two Azerbaijani sailors and injuries to two others.",
    "content": "The Somali-flagged cargo ship TEDY was attacked near the Ukrainian port of Odesa in the Black Sea, according to a report by Euronews. The vessel is registered in Somalia and sails under the Somali flag. The attack resulted in the deaths of two crew members from Azerbaijan. Two other crew members were injured and have been taken to a hospital for treatment.\n\nThe TEDY is owned and operated by Mert Marine Corporation, a Turkish company. Initial reports indicate that the ship first encountered an attack by unmanned aerial vehicles (drones) near Odesa. Subsequently, it was targeted by two additional attacks while it was en route.\n\nThis incident occurs amid ongoing tensions in the Black Sea region, where maritime traffic has been disrupted since the onset of the conflict in Ukraine. The area has seen increased military activity, including the use of drones and other weaponry, affecting commercial shipping.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this event highlights the risks faced by Somali-flagged vessels in international waters, particularly in conflict zones. It also underscores the global nature of shipping, where crew members from various nations, such as Azerbaijan, are employed on ships registered in Somalia.\n\nThe involvement of a Turkish-owned vessel under Somali flag may draw attention to the regulatory and safety challenges for ships registered in Somalia. The Somali diaspora, many of whom have ties to the maritime industry, may be concerned about the implications for Somali seafarers and the reputation of the Somali flag.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/12/markab-ku-gooshayay-calanka-soomaaliya-oo-lagu-weeraray-xeebaha-ukraine/",
